{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"No code changed for this one — it's a design call in cledger's court, so here's the straight assessment.\n\n**Can Codex decrypt it? No — nobody client-side can, including Codex.** The `encrypted_content` blob (~2–4KB per reasoning item, per your own spec doc at `docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md:191-196`) is ciphertext produced *server-side* by OpenAI's Responses API. Codex never decrypts it; it just round-trips the opaque blob back in the next request, and OpenAI's servers decrypt it internally to restore the model's reasoning state. So \"we can't read it\" is true of Codex itself too — but Codex can still *use* it, which is the distinction that matters.\n\n**Is it relevant to the model? Yes, with a gradient.** It matters most *inside* a turn's tool loop — that's where OpenAI explicitly recommends passing reasoning items back, and where dropping them degrades continuity. At a clean user-turn boundary (where turnbridge resumes), prior-turn reasoning is much less load-bearing — the model starts the new turn with fresh reasoning over the visible messages — but it's not provably worthless, and a session interrupted mid-turn is exactly the case where it would matter.\n\n**Your inference is correct, but the blast radius is narrower than it sounds.** Same-machine `codex → codex` never touches cledger: turnbridge routes same-CLI resumes through *native* resume, and the original rollout file still contains every reasoning item. The loss only bites when fabrication is required — resuming on your other machine, a teammate resuming your session, or the original rollout being gone. That's precisely turnbridge's sharing story, so it's a real gap in the product's core promise, not a hypothetical.\n\n**Two more gates sit behind cledger even if it recorded them:**\n\n1. Turnbridge's fabricator omits reasoning items — the spec verified \"omission is safe\" but never tested replaying *real* blobs through a fabricated rollout (it only ruled out synthesizing fake ciphertext). That's a cheap probe: copy a genuine reasoning item into a fabricated session and run the `codex exec resume` marker probe.\n2. **Cross-account validity is undocumented.** The server decrypts under the caller's credentials; whether your teammate's org can replay *your* org's blobs is unknown (and there may be a TTL). Same account on two machines is the likely-works case; teammate sharing needs an empirical answer.\n\n**On the policy question — I think your instinct is right, with one refinement.** The original exclusion conflated \"unreadable by us\" with \"not worth keeping,\" which does contradict the record-everything-JIC posture. The counterarguments are weaker than they look: the secret-scan gate can't scan ciphertext, but the gate exists to keep secrets from *people reading shared notes*, and ciphertext is unreadable to them by construction — the only party who can decrypt already saw the plaintext. The real costs are size (~2–4KB per assistant turn adds up in notes refs) and product framing: cledger sells itself as a record of the *visible* conversation, and this is invisible machine state.\n\nThe refinement: don't put blobs in the visible-content lane. cledger already has exactly the right machinery — the 0.5.0 raw-preservation tier that keeps unmapped source lines in `raw.data`. Record reasoning items that way (or as a dedicated opaque-state kind): excluded from transcripts, titles, and scans, but durably present so a consumer like turnbridge can thread real blobs back into a fabricated rollout for `codex → codex`. Nothing about the visible-record product changes; the JIC principle is honored; and the decision to *use* them stays with the consumer.\n\nIf you want, I can settle the load-bearing unknown right now for a few thousand tokens: inject a real reasoning item from one of your existing rollouts into a fabricated session and probe whether resume accepts it and behaves. If that fails, the cledger debate is moot; if it passes, you have a concrete case for reopening the capture policy.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"353a32db828b8978aa48299ac0e38cc7fe1dd69e","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","seq":527},"id":"ev1-03fd52d27de11f8007549baf881aa0528958c7e59dd6900f52776058eac97110","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-21T23:40:20.491Z","producer":{"session_id":"6b979ed9-e38e-4fea-b585-342bba5a333c","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.9.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"No code changed for this one — it's a design call in cledger's court, so here's the straight assessment.\n\n**Can Codex decrypt it? No — nobody client-side can, including Codex.** The `encrypted_content` blob (~2–4KB per reasoning item, per your own spec doc at `docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md:191-196`) is ciphertext produced *server-side* by OpenAI's Responses API. Codex never decrypts it; it just round-trips the opaque blob back in the next request, and OpenAI's servers decrypt it internally to restore the model's reasoning state. So \"we can't read it\" is true of Codex itself too — but Codex can still *use* it, which is the distinction that matters.\n\n**Is it relevant to the model? Yes, with a gradient.** It matters most *inside* a turn's tool loop — that's where OpenAI explicitly recommends passing reasoning items back, and where dropping them degrades continuity. At a clean user-turn boundary (where turnbridge resumes), prior-turn reasoning is much less load-bearing — the model starts the new turn with fresh reasoning over the visible messages — but it's not provably worthless, and a session interrupted mid-turn is exactly